Fix segment_reduce meta to use data's shape as base (#179361)

The C++ implementation in [`aten/src/ATen/native/SegmentReduce.cpp`](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/aten/src/ATen/native/SegmentReduce.cpp#L143-L145) computes the output shape by starting from data's full shape and replacing only the axis dimension:

```cpp
auto output_shape = data.sizes().vec();
output_shape[axis] = segment_count;
auto output = at::empty(output_shape, data.options());
```

The Python meta kernel was instead using `lengths.shape` as the base:

```python
# Before (wrong) - uses lengths.shape as base, loses data's other dims
def segment_reduce_lengths_tensor(lengths_shape):
    return torch.empty(
        lengths_shape + data.shape[axis + 1 :],
        ...
    )
return segment_reduce_lengths_tensor(lengths.shape)

# After (correct) - uses data.shape as base, replaces only axis dim
def segment_reduce_output(segment_count):
    output_shape = list(data.shape)
    output_shape[axis] = segment_count
    return torch.empty(output_shape, ...)

return segment_reduce_output(lengths.shape[axis])
```

This produced wrong output shapes when the data has dimensions before the reduction axis that differ from lengths' shape. For example, with `data.shape=(10, 5)`, `lengths.shape=(2, 3)`, `axis=1`: the old code gave shape `(2, 3)` instead of the correct `(10, 3)`.
